    {
      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\n**Certificate-level info:**\n- Number: R49/2013-A-CZ1-22.02 (Revision 5)\n- Scheme: A\n- Member state: Czech Republic\n- Date issued: 3 October 2025\n- Page total: 12\n- OIML issuer ID: not explicitly given, but certificate number contains CZ1. Hmm, \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-22.02\" — the CZ1 suggests issuer country code. I'll leave oiml_issuer_id out unless certain. Actually maybe \"CZ1\" is part of the certificate number. I won't fabricate an issuer ID.\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- Name: Czech Metrology Institute\n- Address: Okružní 31, 638 00 Brno, Czech Republic\n- Person responsible: Jan Kalandra\n- Signed by RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body — that's the signature block. The person_responsible on page 1 is \"Jan Kalandra\". I'll use that for person_responsible. Could also mention Pavel Klenovský as person_title... The schema has person_responsible and person_title. Jan Kalandra is listed as \"Person responsible\". Pavel Klenovský signs as Head of Certification Body. I'll put person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\". Maybe add the signing person too — but schema has only one person. I'll keep Jan Kalandra as person_responsible. Actually, could I add \"RNDr. Pavel Klenovský\" somewhere? Not per schema. Maybe I should keep the person responsible as Jan Kalandra only. Hmm, but the signature block includes name and title. The schema fields: person_responsible, person_title. Page 1 says \"Person responsible: Jan Kalandra\" with no title. Page 2: \"The OIML Issuing Authority — RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body\". It's ambiguous. I'll set person_responsible to \"Jan Kalandra\" and not include title, since they're separate entries. Alternatively, could set person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\". I'll do that.\n\n**Applicants:**\n- ISOIL INDUSTRIA SPA, Via F.lli Gracchi, 27; Cinisello Balsamo - MI; 200 99, Italy\n\n**Manufacturers:**\n- Hemina SPA, Via Piemonte 2, Montagnana - PD; 350 44, Italy\n\n**Certified type:**\n- Category: \"Water meter\" (the category examples list \"Gas meter\", \"Measuring system\"... water meter is electromagnetic water meter). Category: \"Water meter\" seems best.\n- Type designations: MS2500 / MV110, MS2500 / MV145, MS2500 / MV210. Also module designations like MS2500 / MV110 HV, etc. The type designations should include the model identifiers: \"MS2500 / MV110\", \"MS2500 / MV145\", \"MS2500 / MV210\".\n- Module designation: The certificate has a \"Designation of the module\" section listing: MS2500 / MV110 HV, MS2500 / MV210 HV + SUPERCAP or Rechargeable Lithium (not replaceable); etc. This is complex. I could set module_designation to a summary string or null. Actually the module designation is quite elaborate — it's combos of converter variants and battery options. Maybe I should capture this as model_family with variants.\n\n**Model family:**\nThe cert covers three converter electronics (MV110, MV210, MV145) × sensor MS2500 across DN 25–2000, plus power supply variants (HV, LV, LLV) and battery options. This is a family. Models could be enumerated per combination. But that would explode. Let me think about what's most useful.\n\nThe basic structure: sensor MS2500 + converter (MV110 | MV210 | MV145). Power supply modifications:\n- MS2500/MV110 HV: mains 100–240 V AC\n- MS2500/MV110 LV: mains 24–36 V AC/DC\n- MS2500/MV110 LLV: mains 12–48 V DC (page 3 says 12–48 V DC; page 7 table says \"10 – 48 V DC\" for LLV. Discrepancy: page 3 says 12–48, page 7 says 10–48. I'll record what's in the table; maybe note. I'll use 12–48 from page 3? Hmm. The power supply table (page 7) says \"10 – 48 V DC\" for the LLV column of MV110/MV210. Page 3 text says 12–48V DC. MV145 LLV table says 12–48 V DC. I'll use 12–48 V DC for LLV consistently? But rule 8 says don't fabricate. Both values exist in the doc. I'll use the detailed spec table value for MV110/MV210 LLV: 10–48 V DC. Actually wait — let me re-read.\n\nPage 3: \"3. The water meter MS2500 / MV110 LLV and MS2500 / MV210 LLV has power supply by main 12 – 48V DC.\"\nPage 7 table: Type row: \"100 – 240 V AC\" | \"24 – 36V AC/DC\" | \"10 – 48 V DC\"\n\nThese conflict. I'll go with the spec table (10–48 V DC) for the model_level attribute since it's the detailed specification, but it could be an OCR issue. Hmm. Risky either way. I'll pick 10–48 for MV110/MV210 LLV as per table. Actually, let me think — MV145 LLV is 12–48 V DC per the MV145 table. The MV110/MV210 LLV being 10–48 is plausible as printed. I'll use 10–48 for that one.\n\nModels: I think a sensible model enumeration:\n- MS2500/MV110 HV\n- MS2500/MV110 LV\n- MS2500/MV110 LLV\n- MS2500/MV210 HV\n- MS2500/MV210 LV\n- MS2500/MV210 LLV\n- MS2500/MV145 HV\n- MS2500/MV145 LLV\n\nWith battery attributes: MV145 HV and/or Lithium or Alkaline Batteries (replaceable); MV145 LLV and/or Lithium or Alkaline (replaceable). MV110/MV210: SUPERCAP or Rechargeable Lithium (not replaceable) — internal backup.\n\nHmm, the model_family could be: family_name: \"MS2500\" (the sensor family) or \"MS2500 / MV110; MS2500 / MV210; MS2500 / MV145\". I'd say family_name: \"MS2500\" — but that's the sensor. The models are combinations. Let me use family_name: null and models as the 8 combos, each with attributes for power supply type, voltage, frequency, backup source, software version, CRC.\n\nActually the attributes per model:\nMV110/MV210 (HV): power supply 100–240 V AC, frequency 45–66 Hz, internal backup 3.7 V DC 5200 mAh battery, min battery life: SUPER CAP 1 minute / Rechargeable Lithium 15 days at reference conditions, SW V.1.05.0000.0004 / V.1.06.0000.0004, CRC F2476CEF / 40FF6355, cut-off 0.2% of Q3, empty pipe detection on.\nMV110/MV210 (LV): 24–36 V AC/DC, freq 0 (DC)…45–66 Hz, same backup etc.\nMV110/MV210 (LLV): 10–48 V DC (page 7) / 12–48 (page 3), freq 0 (DC), same.\nMV145 (HV): 100–240 V AC, freq 45–66 Hz, backup 3.6 V Lithium / 9 V Alkaline, battery life Lithium 3 years (1+1) / Alkaline 1 year (nine) in SMART mode, SW V.1.06.0000.0005 / 1.07.0000.0005, CRC EF726D9C / A1D8736F, cut-off 0.2% Q3, empty pipe detection on.\nMV145 (LLV): 12–48 V DC, freq DC, same backup etc.\n\nNote: no MV145 LV exists.\n\nSo models list:\n1. MS2500/MV110 HV\n2. MS2500/MV110 LV\n3. MS2500/MV110 LLV\n4. MS2500/MV210 HV\n5. MS2500/MV210 LV\n6. MS2500/MV210 LLV\n7. MS2500/MV145 HV\n8. MS2500/MV145 LLV\n\nThe power-supply spec tables actually group MV110 and MV210 together (same spec). So maybe models should be grouped: \"MS2500 / MV110 HV; MS2500 / MV210 HV\" etc. But for model_level attributes, the model identifier could be the module designation. Given the tables treat MV110 and MV210 identically, I could define models as the module designations:\n- \"MS2500/MV110 HV\" and \"MS2500/MV210 HV\" share attributes...\n\nI think a cleaner approach: models = 8 variants as above, and in model_level attributes use those model IDs with values repeated. That's verbose but accurate. Alternatively, define model-level axes at the \"power supply variant\" level. The schema wants model_id per variant. I'll do 8 variants and for attributes that differ only by variant class (HV/LV/LLV), repeat values.\n\nActually wait — should MV110 and MV210 be separate models at all? The sensor is the same MS2500; the converter differs (MV110 vs MV210 vs MV145). Yes, MV210 is new in Revision 5. So converter choice is a model dimension.\n\nHmm, but maybe simpler: treat the DN (nominal diameter) as a configuration axis rather than models, since flowrates vary per DN and per class. The flowrate tables are DN × class matrices — that's a config-level matrix (axis: nominal diameter + accuracy class).\n\nLet me structure:\n\nmodel_family.models: 8 entries as above.\n\ncharacteristics.type_level (whole-cert):\n- q3_over_q1_ratio: 400 for class 2; 250 for class 1 — this varies by class and DN. Hmm. Put as config_level with axis \"accuracy_class and nominal_diameter\"? Or keep in matrix table. The flowrate tables capture R per DN per class. I'll put ratio in matrix tables and reference.\n- q2_over_q1: 1.6\n- q4_over_q3: 1.25\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic water meter\"\n- accuracy_class: [\"1\", \"2\"] — hmm, value should be single. Could be a string \"1 and 2\". Or config_level? I'll put \"1, 2\" as a string value? The schema says value: number | {min,max} | string. \"1 and 2\" as string is acceptable.\n- mpe_lower_zone: ±3% class 1, ±5% class 2 → config_level with axis accuracy_class: {condition \"class 1\", value 3 unit \"%\"}, {condition \"class 2\", value 5, \"%\"}. Or type_level as string. I'll use config_level.\n- mpe_upper_zone: ±1% (class 1 in-flow?) — wait, R49 MPE: for upper zone, ±1% for class 1 water meters... Actually the table says \"±1 (class 1) ±2 (class 2) % / ±2 (class1) ±3 (class 2) %\" — the second line probably applies to flowrates below 0.2·Q3? Hmm, actually R49-1: in upper zone Q2≤Q≤Q4: MPE ±1% class1, ±2% class2 for Q3≥... hmm, temperature. Actually R49: for high zones: class 1: ±1%, class 2: ±2%; but for flow rates below... hmm the two-line listing: first line for temperatures 30°C–50°C? Actually R49-1 2013: MPE for high flow zone is ±1% (class 1) and ±2% (class 2) when temperature ≤ 30°C, and ±2%/±3% for >30°C? No wait, that's backwards. Let me not overthink — the table lists two rows of MPE values. I'll represent as two config entries or keep as strings. Simpler: type_level with value string: \"±1 % (class 1) / ±2 % (class 2); ±2 % (class 1) / ±3 % (class 2)\". Hmm, but strict value rules... The MPE entries are multi-line. I could do config_level:\n\nconfig_level entries:\n- attribute: \"maximum_permitted_error_upper_zone\", axis: \"accuracy_class / temperature\", conditions... this is getting messy. \n\nSimplest faithful approach: put MPE values as type_level strings with the exact text, minus footnote markers. Values:\n- \"±3 % (Class 1); ±5 % (Class 2)\" for lower zone. Hmm, but value should not have units embedded ideally... MPE is in % — value: {min:3,max:5}? No.\n\nI think it's acceptable: value as string \"±3 % (class 1); ±5 % (class 2)\" with unit_symbol \"%\"? Not great. Alternative: config_level:\n- attribute \"maximum_permitted_error_lower_zone\", axis \"accuracy_class\", values: [{condition \"class 1\", value 3, unit \"%\"}, {condition \"class 2\", value 5, unit \"%\"}] — but MPE is ±3, so value 3 with a note? I could record value: 3 and unit \"%\" and it's understood as max permissible error magnitude. The ± sign... I'd keep value as string \"±3\" maybe. Hmm, value rules: numbers not strings. But \"±3\" is signed. I'll use value: 3 and unit \"%\" — the attribute name says max permissible error, sign convention understood. Or value: \"±3%\". Let me just use numbers: lower zone class1: 3, class2: 5. Upper zone: for accuracy class axis plus a second axis dimension (two rows). The two rows in the doc: first row \"±1 (class 1) ±2 (class 2) %\" and second \"±2 (class1) ±3 (class 2) %\". In R49, upper zone MPE: ±1%/±2% applies; the second row probably for volume... Hmm, in R49-1:2013, Table 2: water meters class 1: high zones ±1%, low zones ±3%; class 2: high ±2%, low ±5%. Where does ±2/±3% come from? Maybe for temperatures above 30 °C (T90 hot water). Actually yes! R49-1:2013 clause 6.1: \"For water meters of accuracy class 1 and 2, the MPEs are: ... for water temperature ≤ 30 °C: ±1%/±3% (class1)... for temperature > 30 °C: ±2%/±3% class 1 and ±3%/±5% class 2\"? Hmm I recall: Class 1: high zones: ±1% (T≤30°C), ±2% (T>30°C); low zones ±3%. Class 2: high zones ±2% (T≤30), ±3% (T>30); low zones ±5%. Yes that matches: row 1: ±1 (class1) ±2 (class2) % for T≤30°C; row 2: ±2 (class1) ±3 (class2) % for T>30°C. The cert doesn't state the condition though. I'll represent as config_level with axis \"accuracy_class_and_temperature_condition\" — risky to fabricate the temperature condition. Better: keep the raw two-row structure. I could put it in matrix_tables as \"Maximum permissible error\" table? That seems overkill.\n\nDecision: type_level:\n- \"mpe_lower_flowrate_zone\": value: \"±3 % (class 1); ±5 % (class 2)\" — as string with unit null? Or value \"±3/±5\"? I'll use value: \"±3 (class 1); ±5 (class 2)\", unit_symbol: \"%\". Hmm — the strict rules say numbers where possible, but this is genuinely multi-valued conditional. Strings allowed for formulas/symbols. I think string is fine here.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: config_level with axis \"accuracy_class\":\n- attribute: maximum_permitted_error_lower_flowrate_zone, axis: accuracy_class, values: [{condition: \"class 1\", value: \"±3\", unit \"%\"}, {condition: \"class 2\", value: \"±5\", unit \"%\"}]\n\nAnd upper zone: axis \"accuracy_class (two temperature rows)\" — no. I'll do:\n- attribute: maximum_permitted_error_upper_flowrate_zone, axis: \"accuracy_class / condition\", values: [{condition: \"class 1 (row 1)\", ...}] — fabrication of condition.\n\nSimplest compliant: type_level strings. I'll go with:\n- \"maximum_permitted_error_lower_flowrate_zone\": {value: \"±3 (class 1); ±5 (class 2)\", unit_symbol: \"%\"}\n- \"maximum_permitted_error_upper_flowrate_zone\": {value: \"±1 (class 1); ±2 (class 2) / ±2 (class 1); ±3 (class 2)\", unit_symbol: \"%\"}\n\nThat preserves info without fabricating.\n\n- temperature_class: varies by liner → config_level with axis \"liner\":\n  values: [{condition: \"PTFE liner\", value: \"T90; T50; T30\"}, {condition: \"Rilsan liner\", value: \"T50; T30 (from DN50)\"}, {condition: \"Ebonite liner\", value: \"T50; T30 (from DN200)\"}, {condition: \"polypropylene liner\", value: \"T50; T30\"}]. Values as strings (T90 etc. are class codes, fine as strings).\n- max_admissible_temperature: config_level axis \"liner\": PTFE → 90 °C; others → 50 °C.\n- water_pressure_class: \"MAP40\"\n- pressure_loss_class: \"Δp40\"\n- reverse_flow: \"designed to measure\"\n- environmental_class: \"B, O\"\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E1; E2\"\n- ambient_temperature_range: {min:-25, max:55}, \"°C\"\n- max_admissible_pressure: 4, \"MPa\"\n- orientation_limitation: \"any\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- installation_sensitivity_class: \"U0 D0\" (from markings: \"Ux Dx – it does not differ from U0 D0\")\n- connection_type: \"Flanged\"\n- min_straight_length_inlet_pipe: 0 mm\n- min_straight_length_outlet_pipe: 0 mm\n- flow_conditioner: \"No\"\n- case (EUT): \"D\"\n- category: \"Electromagnetic water meter\"\n\n**DN-dependent (config axis: nominal diameter):**\n- Indicating range (testing/user mode) [m³]: DN25–350: 99 999 / 999 999 / 999 999 / 9 999 999 across 4 DN column groups; DN400–2000: 999 999 999.\n  Column groups for DN25–350: (25;32;40;50) → 99 999; (65;80) → 999 999; (100;125;150) → 999 999; (200;250;300;350) → 9 999 999.\n- Resolution of indicating device [m³]: DN25–350: 0.0001 / 0.001 / 0.001 / 0.01; DN400–2000: 0.01 (0.1 for DN600), 0.1 (700–1000), 1 (1200–2000). Columns: (400;450;500;600) → \"0.01 / 0.1 (DN600)\"; (700–1000) → 0.1; (1200–2000) → 1.\n- Length [mm]: DN25–350: 200 / 200 / 250-250-300 / 350-450-500-550; DN400+: 600 / 700-800-900-1000 / 1200-1400-1500-1600 / 1800-2000.\n\nThese are DN-dependent — config_level with axis \"nominal_diameter_group\" or better: matrix_tables. Given the multi-dimensional nature (DN × class for flowrates), I'll emit:\n\nmatrix_tables:\n1. \"Basic metrological data (flowrates) — Class 1\": columns: dn, r, q4, q3, q2, q1; rows for DN 25–600 with class 1 R=250 values.\n2. \"Basic metrological data (flowrates) — Class 2\": DN 25–2000, R per the special table (400 up to DN600; DN700 R250; DN800/900 R160; DN1000 R100; DN1200–1600 R80; DN1800/2000 R40).\n\nWait — class 1 table only goes to DN 600 (rows 25..600). The class 2 table spans 25–2000. That matches the doc: class 1 table ends at DN600 (page 8), class 2 continues to 2000 (page 9). Interesting — for class 1, ratios beyond DN600 aren't listed (probably class 1 only up to DN600? The type details for DN400-2000 say Q3/Q1 \"80 for class 2\" and \"40 for class 2\" for 1800-2000 — no class 1 mention. And for DN700-1000 \"for class 2\". So class 1 is only certified up to DN600.) I'll capture exactly what's there.\n\nClass 1 rows:\nDN25: R250, Q4 20, Q3 16, Q2 0.10, Q1 0.064\nDN32: 250, 31, 25, 0.16, 0.10\nDN40: 250, 50, 40, 0.26, 0.16\nDN50: 250, 78.75, 63, 0.40, 0.25\nDN65: 250, 125, 100, 0.64, 0.40\nDN80: 250, 200, 160, 1.0, 0.64\nDN100: 250, 312.5, 250, 1.6, 1.0\nDN125: 250, 500, 400, 2.6, 1.6\nDN150: 250, 787.5, 630, 4.0, 2.5\nDN200: 250, 1250, 1000, 6.4, 4.0\nDN250: 250, 2000, 1600, 10, 6.4\nDN300: 250, 3125, 2500, 16, 10.0\nDN350: 250, 3125, 2500, 16, 10.0\nDN400: 250, 5000, 4000, 26, 16\nDN450: 250, 5000, 4000, 26, 16\nDN500: 250, 7875, 6300, 40, 25\nDN600: 250, 12500, 10000, 64, 40\n\nClass 2 rows:\nDN25: 400, 20, 16, 0.06, 0.040\nDN32: 400, 31, 25, 0.10, 0.063\nDN40: 400, 50, 40, 0.16, 0.10\nDN50: 400, 78.75, 63, 0.25, 0.16\nDN65: 400, 125, 100, 0.40, 0.25\nDN80: 400, 200, 160, 0.64, 0.40\nDN100: 400, 312.5, 250, 1.00, 0.63\nDN125: 400, 500, 400, 1.60, 1.0\nDN150: 400, 787.5, 630, 2.52, 1.6\nDN200: 400, 1250, 1000, 4.00, 2.5\nDN250: 400, 2000, 1600, 6.40, 4.0\nDN300: 400, 3125, 2500, 10, 6.3\nDN350: 400, 3125, 2500, 10, 6.3\nDN400: 400, 5000, 4000, 16, 10\nDN450: 400, 5000, 4000, 16, 10\nDN500: 400, 7875, 6300, 25, 16\nDN600: 400, 12500, 10000, 40, 25\nDN700: 250, 12500, 10000, 64, 40\nDN800: 160, 20000, 16000, 160, 100\nDN900: 160, 20000, 16000, 160, 100\nDN1000: 100, 20000, 16000, 256, 160\nDN1200: 80, 20000, 16000, 320, 200\nDN1400: 80, 20000, 16000, 320, 200\nDN1500: 80, 20000, 16000, 320, 200\nDN1600: 80, 20000, 16000, 320, 200\nDN1800: 40, 20000, 16000, 640, 400\nDN2000: 40, 20000, 16000, 640, 400\n\nAlso the DN-dependent \"type details\" for Q3/Q1 ratio on page 6: DN400-600: 400 class 2 / 250 class 1; DN700: R250; DN800: R160; DN900: R160; DN1000: R100 (class 2); DN1200-1600: 80 class 2; DN1800-2000: 40 class 2. Consistent with the class-2 flowrate table.\n\nNow, model_level vs config_level: the DN is not a \"model\" per se; it's a size. The schema example for R137 treats sizes as models. Hmm. In the R137 example, \"Size G1.6, G2.5\" → models. Here, DN 25–2000 sizes with a full matrix per class. I could treat DN as config axis and put the flowrates in matrix_tables only, or treat DN as model dimension. The guidance: config_level for \"attribute × config-axis matrix\" e.g., static vs dynamic. DN is like a size — similar to R137 size. But 43 DN×class combos would explode model_level entries. The matrix_tables preserve it best. I'll put flowrates in matrix_tables (two tables, one per class) and also add config_level entries for the DN-group-dependent attributes (indicating range, resolution, length) with axis \"nominal_diameter\".\n\nFor config_level entries like:\n- attribute: \"indicating_range\", axis: \"nominal_diameter\", values: [{condition: \"DN 25–50\", value: 99999, unit \"m³\"}, {condition: \"DN 65–80\", value: 999999}, {condition: \"DN 100–150\", value: 999999}, {condition: \"DN 200–350\", value: 9999999}, {condition: \"DN 400–2000\", value: 999999999}]\n\nHmm wait, DN25-350 columns: (25;32;40;50)→99999; (65;80)→999999; (100;125;150)→999999; (200;250;300;350)→9999999. And DN400-2000 → 999999999. Values like 99 999 → 99999. OK.\n\n- attribute: \"indicating_device_resolution\", axis: \"nominal_diameter\": DN25-50: 0.0001; DN65-80: 0.001; DN100-150: 0.001; DN200-350: 0.01; DN400-500: 0.01; DN600: 0.1; DN700-1000: 0.1; DN1200-2000: 1. Wait the DN400-2000 table: columns (400;450;500;600) → \"0.01 / 0.1 (DN600)\" — so 400-500: 0.01, 600: 0.1; (700–1000) → 0.1; (1200–1600; 1800; 2000) → 1 (colspan 2 covers last two groups).\n\n- attribute: \"length\", axis: \"nominal_diameter\": DN25-50: 200; DN65-80: 200; DN100-150: 250; DN200-350: 350/450/500/550 (varies within group — the cell \"250/250/300\" corresponds to DN100/125/150; \"350/450/500/550\" to DN200/250/300/350). So actually per-DN lengths: DN100: 250, DN125: 250, DN150: 300; DN200: 350, DN250: 450, DN300: 500, DN350: 550. And DN400: 600; DN700: 700, DN800: 800, DN900: 900, DN1000: 1000; DN1200: 1200, DN1400: 1400, DN1500: 1500, DN1600: 1600; DN1800: 1800, DN2000: 2000. The slash-separated values map positionally to the DNs in the group. For DN400 group \"600\" single value for (400;450;500;600) — so 600 for all of 400-600? The cell is \"600\" for group 400;450;500;600 → length 600 for each. For DN65-80 → 200; DN25-50 → 200.\n\nI could expand config values per DN group with slash lists preserved as strings: {condition: \"DN 100; 125; 150\", value: \"250/250/300\"}. Better: keep groups with slash strings to avoid mis-assigning. Actually positional mapping is clear: 3 values ↔ 3 DNs. I could expand: DN100→250, DN125→250, DN150→300, DN200→350, DN250→450, DN300→500, DN350→550. That's faithful. For DN400-600: \"600\" for all. DN700-1000: \"700/800/900/1000\" → DN700:700 etc. DN1200-1600: \"1200/1400/1500/1600\" → per DN. DN1800-2000: \"1800/2000\".\n\nI'll expand these — it's cleaner and faithful.\n\nSimilarly resolution for DN groups: DN25-50: 0.0001 (single value for group of 4 — same for all), DN65-80: 0.001, DN100-150: 0.001, DN200-350: 0.01, DN400-450-500: 0.01, DN600: 0.1, DN700-1000: 0.1, DN1200-2000: 1.\n\nAnd indicating range: DN25-50: 99999, DN65-80: 999999, DN100-150: 999999, DN200-350: 9999999, DN400-2000: 999999999.\n\nNow **model_level**: what varies across the 8 model variants? Power supply type/voltage/frequency, backup source, battery life, software version, CRC. Let me build:\n\nModels (module designations):\n- \"MS2500/MV110 HV\" — power_supply: {min:100,max:240} V AC; frequency 45–66 Hz; backup: rechargeable lithium 3.7V DC 5200mAh (SUPERCAP or rechargeable lithium); SW: V.1.05.0000.0004 / V.1.06.0000.0004; CRC: F2476CEF / 40FF6355.\n- \"MS2500/MV110 LV\" — 24–36 V AC/DC; freq 0 (DC) … 45–66 Hz.\n- \"MS2500/MV110 LLV\" — 10–48 V DC; freq 0 (DC).\n- Same for MV210.\n- \"MS2500/MV145 HV\" — 100–240 V AC; 45–66 Hz; backup 3.6 V Lithium / 9 V Alkaline (replaceable); SW V.1.06.0000.0005 / 1.07.0000.0005; CRC EF726D9C / A1D8736F.\n- \"MS2500/MV145 LLV\" — 12–48 V DC; DC; same backup/SW.\n\nTo avoid duplicating values across MV110 and MV210 identical rows, maybe I define model_level \"model\" identifiers as the shared groups? E.g. \"MS2500/MV110 HV; MS2500/MV210 HV\". That's how the doc groups them. Hmm — but then models list has 8 entries while attribute rows reference grouped strings. That's inconsistent but acceptable? I think cleaner: model_level entries where \"model\" field is the module designation group exactly as the doc: e.g. model: \"MS2500/MV110 HV\" and another row for \"MS2500/MV210 HV\". Repetition is fine and machine-friendly.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether to define models as 8 or as combined groups of 5:\nThe doc's module designation section lists:\n1. MS2500/MV110 HV, MS2500/MV210 HV + SUPERCAP or Rechargeable Lithium (not replaceable)\n2. MS2500/MV110 LV, MS2500/MV210 LV + SUPERCAP or Rechargeable Lithium (not replaceable)\n3. MS2500/MV110 LLV, MS2500/MV210 LLV + SUPERCAP or Rechargeable Lithium (not replaceable)\n4. MS2500/MV145 HV and/or Lithium or Alkaline Batteries (replaceable)\n5. MS2500/MV145 LLV and/or Lithium or Alkaline Batteries (replaceable)\n\nSo 5 module-designation groups covering 8 model variants. I'll enumerate 8 models with attributes:\n\nmodels:\n1. {model_id: \"MS2500/MV110 HV\", attributes: {power_supply: \"mains AC\", backup_source: \"SUPERCAP or rechargeable lithium (not replaceable)\"}}\n... \n\nMaybe simpler to put battery info in attributes of each model and keep model_level characteristic entries for power supply voltage, frequency, software version, CRC, backup source voltage, battery life.\n\nLet me draft model_level entries:\n\n1. power_supply_voltage: values:\n - {model: \"MS2500/MV110 HV\", value: {min:100,max:240}, unit \"V\"}\n - MV110 LV: {min:24,max:36} V\n - MV110 LLV: {min:10,max:48} V (page 7) — conflict with page 3 (12–48). Hmm. Which to record? Both appear. The detailed table is more specific for these models; I'll use 10–48. But hmm, this could be an OCR artifact of \"12\" → \"10\"? Page 3 says 12–48V DC for MV110/MV210 LLV; MV145 LLV spec says 12–48. A 10–48 seems odd. But rule: don't fabricate — I must pick what's written. The spec table explicitly says \"10 – 48 V DC\". I'll go with 10–48 and could add a footnote? I can't fabricate footnotes. I'll just use 10–48 from the spec table. Hmm, alternatively use 12–48 from the descriptive text. Ugh.\n\nLet me think about which is more likely the true value: MV145 LLV is 12–48 V DC (both page 3 and page 7 table agree). MV110/MV210 LLV: page 3 says 12–48; page 7 says 10–48. One is an OCR/typo. Given two sources for 12–48 (MV145 table + page 3 text) vs one for 10–48, 12–48 is more plausible as the true value. But the instruction is to extract what's in the markdown; the markdown contains both. I'll go with the dedicated specification table value \"10 – 48 V DC\" for MV110/MV210 LLV since that's the authoritative-looking spec table... Actually no — I'll go with 12–48 for consistency with page 3 text which explicitly names MS2500/MV110 LLV and MS2500/MV210 LLV. Hmm.\n\nHonestly either is defensible. The page 3 text: \"The water meter MS2500 / MV110 LLV and MS2500 / MV210 LLV has power supply by main 12 – 48V DC.\" — explicit per-model statement. The table might have OCR error. I'll use 12–48. Decision made.\n\n - MV145 HV: {min:100,max:240} V AC\n - MV145 LLV: {min:12,max:48} V DC\n\n2. power_supply_type: HV: \"AC\"; LV: \"AC/DC\"; LLV: \"DC\". Could be part of voltage value... I'll add a separate entry: power_supply_type: {model, value: \"AC mains\"} etc. Or fold into attributes of models. I'll include as model_level attribute \"power_supply_type\" with values AC / AC/DC / DC.\n\n3. power_supply_frequency: HV: {min:45,max:66} Hz; LV: {min:0,max:66} Hz (0 DC … 45–66 Hz — mixed DC/AC; the doc: \"0 (DC) …45 – 66 Hz\"); LLV: 0 (DC). For LLV, value 0, unit Hz. For LV: value string \"0 (DC) … 45–66 Hz\"? Ranges with strings... I'll record: LV: {min: 0, max: 66} Hz? That misstates (it's DC or 45-66Hz AC). Better: value: \"0 (DC) / 45–66 Hz\" as string, unit Hz. For LLV: 0 Hz (DC). HV: {min:45,max:66} Hz.\n\nHmm, strict value rules want numbers. But \"0 (DC) … 45–66 Hz\" is genuinely a list. I'll use strings where needed: value \"DC or 45–66 Hz\"? Let me keep close to doc: LV: \"0 (DC)…45–66 Hz\", LLV: \"0 (DC)\", HV: {min:45,max:66}.\n\n4. internal_backup_source: MV110/MV210: \"3.7 V DC, 5200 mAh battery (SUPERCAP or rechargeable lithium)\". MV145: \"3.6 V lithium / 9 V alkaline\". This is more a description than a characteristic. Could split:\n - backup_source_voltage: MV110/MV210 variants: 3.7 V DC; MV145: 3.6 V (lithium) or 9 V (alkaline) DC.\n I'll do: internal_backup_source: value strings per model.\n\n5. minimum_battery_life_time_internal_backup: MV110/MV210: \"SUPERCAP: 1 minute / rechargeable lithium: 15 days at reference conditions\"; MV145: \"Lithium: 3 years (1+1) / Alkaline: 1 year (nine), in SMART mode under proper environmental and working conditions\". Strings.\n\n6. software_version: MV110/MV210: \"V.1.05.0000.0004, V.1.06.0000.0004\"; MV145: \"V.1.06.0000.0005, V.1.07.0000.0005\".\n\n7. crc_checksum_crc32: MV110/MV210: \"F2476CEF, 40FF6355\"; MV145: \"EF726D9C, A1D8736F\".\n\n8. cut_off: \"0.2 % of Q3\" for all models → could be type_level: value 0.2, unit \"%\", reference Q3. I'll do type_level: {value: 0.2, unit_symbol: \"%\"} plus... the \"of Q₃\" reference. Rule example: `safe_overload_reference: {value: \"Emax\"}` — analogous: cut_off_reference: \"Q3\". I'll add cut_off: {value: 0.2, unit \"%\"} and cut_off_reference: {value: \"Q3\"}. Hmm, or value string \"0.2 % of Q3\". I'll do the split approach per the rule example.\n\n9. empty_pipe_detection: \"on\" — type_level string \"on\". Or boolean true. I'll use \"on\" as string? \"enabled\"? Keep \"on\".\n\n**Components:**\n- role: \"Sensor (measurement transducer)\", type_designations: [\"MS2500\"]\n- role: \"Converter (electronic indicating device/calculating device)\", type_designations: [\"MV110\", \"MV210\", \"MV145\"]\nThe doc: \"consist of sensor and converter water meter in the compact set up or separate version\". Sensor MS2500 with liners; converters MV110/MV210/MV145. I'll add components entries.\n\nAlso liner options could be characteristics of sensor component. Components entry characteristics: sensor liners: PTFE (DN25–2000, 90°C), Polypropylene (DN25–150, 50°C), Rilsan (DN50–2000, 50°C), Ebonite (DN200–2000, 50°C). I'll include as component characteristics strings or a small matrix. I'll put a compact description in the component's characteristics.\n\n**Recommendation:**\nid: \"R49\", edition: 2013 — wait, edition (int). \"Edition (year): 2013\" → edition: 2013? The schema says edition (int). The year is 2013. R49 is the recommendation; edition year 2013. I'll put edition: 2013. Hmm, typically edition might be like \"2013\". Yes int 2013. accuracy_classes: [\"1\", \"2\"] — or [1, 2]. I'll use [\"1\",\"2\"] strings since class labels. The example in schema: accuracy_classes[]. I'll use [\"1\", \"2\"]. Scheme: \"A\".\n\nAmendment: none → omit.\n\n**test_reports:**\n- id: \"0511-ER-V097-24\", date: 2025-09-30, pages: 268, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"\n- id: \"6015-PT-P0029-25\", pages: 159, role: \"test_report\" (date unknown)\n- id: \"6011-PT-SW012-22\", pages: 6\n- id: \"6011-PT-SW009-22\", pages: 9\n- id: \"6011-PT-SW010-23\", pages: 6\n- id: \"6011-PT-SW017-25\", pages: 6\n- id: \"8551-PT-E0261-25\", pages: 44\nAlso documentation file: \"0511-UL-V097-24\" → test_reports entry with role \"documentation_file\"? The schema lists documentation_file as a role under test_reports. Yes.\n\nDates: only the type evaluation report has a date (30 Sept 2025). Date format: \"2025-09-30\". The cert date_issued: 2025-10-03.\n\n**revision_history:**\n- {revision: \"0\", date: \"2022-05-26\", changes: \"Issuing certificate\"}\n- {revision: \"1\", date: \"2022-09-13\", changes: \"Adding electronics MV145\"}\n- {revision: \"2\", date: \"2023-02-02\", changes: \"Corrected the battery parameters in the electronics MV145 and corrected adding MV145 for DN>=200 mm\"}\n- {revision: \"3\", date: \"2023-10-20\", changes: \"Extension up to DN 2000\"}\n- {revision: \"4\", date: \"2024-05-28\", changes: \"Change of the OIML Test report No. 6015-PT-P5005-24\"}\n- {revision: \"5\", date: \"2025-10-03\", changes: \"extension to PN40, new type MS2500 / MV210, new SW and CRC version, replaceable batteries and editorial changes.\"}\n\n**footnotes:** Page 7 has superscript 1 on \"Electromagnetic environment: 1\" and footnote text: \"1) The ratio Q₃/Q₁ shall be chosen according to paragraph 4.1.4 of OIML R 49-1:2013\". Hmm wait — the footnote marker is attached to \"Electromagnetic environment\" row on page 6 table (`Electromagnetic environment: <sup>1</sup>`), and footnote on page 7. The footnote text is about Q3/Q1 ratio choice. Odd but that's what the doc says. Record footnotes: [{marker: \"1\", text: \"The ratio Q3/Q1 shall be chosen according to paragraph 4.1.4 of OIML R 49-1:2013\"}]. And attach footnote_markers [\"1\"] to the electromagnetic_environment type_level value? That seems to be where the marker appears. Hmm, semantically it belongs to the ratio table maybe, but I'll attach to electromagnetic_environment as printed. Actually wait, is that weird? Marker on \"Electromagnetic environment\" but text about Q3/Q1 — likely a layout artifact (footnote marker placed near bottom of page). I'll attach it to electromagnetic_environment per the doc placement.\n\n**matrix_tables:** the two flowrate tables. Columns: dn, r_q3_over_q1, q4, q3, q2, q1. Units: dn in mm (DN nominal diameter), flowrates m³/h. Include column_units.\n\nAlso maybe a third matrix table for the DN-group technical data (length, indicating range, resolution)? I've put those into config_level. Good enough.\n\n**certificate fields:**\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-22.02\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: not present → omit.\n- page_total: 12\n- member_state: \"Czech Republic\"\n- date_issued: \"2025-10-03\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: not explicitly stated → omit. Hmm, certificate number contains \"CZ1\". Some CMI certs have issuer ID CZ1... not stated as such. Omit.\n\nRevision: \"Revision 5\" — schema doesn't have a revision field at certificate level except revision_history. Maybe add \"revision\": \"5\"? Not in schema. The schema has revision_history. I'll include the current revision implicitly via revision_history. Or add \"revision\": \"5\" — rule 8 says omit fields not present in schema... but extra fields probably tolerated. I'll skip to be safe. Actually maybe include it in certificate? The schema yaml doesn't list it. Skip.\n\n**characteristics.type_level** final list:\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic water meter\"\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\"? or values [1,2]? I'll do value: \"1; 2\". Hmm strings allowed. OK.\n- q2_over_q1: 1.6 (dimensionless)\n- q4_over_q3: 1.25\n- q3_over_q1: varies by class and DN → I'll do config_level axis \"nominal_diameter and accuracy_class\"? Better covered by matrix tables. But a summary entry helps: config_level: attribute \"q3_over_q1_ratio\", axis \"accuracy_class\", values: [{condition: \"class 1 (DN 25–600)\", value: 250}, {condition: \"class 2 (DN 25–600)\", value: 400}, {condition: \"class 2 (DN 700)\", value: 250}, {condition: \"class 2 (DN 800; 900)\", value: 160}, {condition: \"class 2 (DN 1000)\", value: 100}, {condition: \"class 2 (DN 1200–1600)\", value: 80}, {condition: \"class 2 (DN 1800; 2000)\", value: 40}]. That captures the page 6 table exactly. \n\n- mpe entries as discussed.\n- temperature_class config by liner.\n- water_pressure_class: \"MAP40\" (string)\n- pressure_loss_class: \"Δp40\"\n- reverse_flow: \"designed to measure\"\n- environmental_class: \"B, O\"\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E1; E2\" with footnote_markers [\"1\"]\n- ambient_temperature_range: {min:-25, max:55} °C\n- max_admissible_temperature config by liner: PTFE 90 °C; polypropylene/Rilsan/Ebonite 50 °C\n- max_admissible_pressure: 4 MPa\n- orientation: \"any\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- installation_sensitivity_class: \"U0 D0\"\n- connection_type: \"Flanged\"\n- min straight lengths: 0 mm each\n- flow_conditioner_required: \"No\"\n- eut_category: \"Electromagnetic water meter\"\n- eut_case: \"D\"\n- volume_resolution_display: \"0.001 m³\" (from description: displays volume resolution of 0.001 m³ on digital display) — could include. I'll add display_volume_resolution: 0.001 m³.\n- communication_interfaces: \"impulse output, current output; RS485 or USB mini-B (legally non-relevant)\" — description-ish. Maybe put in certified_type.description instead. The description field: I'll write a concise summary.\n- cut_off: 0.2 % of Q3 → cut_off {value:0.2, unit:\"%\"}, cut_off_reference {value:\"Q3\"}\n- empty_pipe_detection: \"on\"\n\n**config_level** entries (axis: nominal_diameter):\n- indicating_range: values per groups (m³)\n- indicating_device_resolution: per groups (m³)\n- length: per DN (mm)\n\n**model_level** entries as drafted.\n\nLet me also double check the \"Type\" of power supply naming: HV = \"100 – 240 V AC\"; LV = \"24 – 36V AC/DC\"; LLV per table \"10 – 48 V DC\" vs page 3 \"12 – 48V DC\". I decided 12–48. Hmm, wait once more... Rule 8 \"Don't fabricate\" — using 12–48 is from page 3 explicit statement for those models. Fine.\n\nMV145 HV backup: \"internal backup source 9V DC alk batteries\" / \"3,6V DC lithium\" — and spec: \"3.6 V / Lithium / 9V Alkaline\". OK.\n\nFrequency MV145 LLV: \"DC\" → value \"DC\" string, unit null. MV145 HV: 45–66 Hz.\n\n**Battery life strings**: keep as strings.\n\n**Software/CRC**: two versions each; keep as strings with both values.\n\nNow components characteristics: liners per DN/temperature — I'll include in sensor component characteristics:\nliners: [{material PTFE, dn range 25–2000, water temp 90°C}, ...] — but characteristics{} is free-form object. I'll write:\n\"liners\": \"PTFE (DN 25–DN 2000, water temperature up to 90 °C); Polypropylene (DN 25–DN 150, 50 °C); Rilsan (DN 50–DN 2000, 50 °C); Ebonite (DN 200–DN 2000, 50 °C)\". As strings. Fine.\n\ncertified_type.description: summary from page 3.\n\n**Category**: \"Water meter\". Good.\n\n**type_designations**: [\"MS2500 / MV110\", \"MS2500 / MV145\", \"MS2500 / MV210\"]. Also sensor \"MS2500\" and converters listed separately in components. Maybe include just the three combined designations. Also \"MS2500-P80-B1A2B\" from label is an example label content (Mod field) — not a certified type designation; skip.\n\n**module_designation**: The cert has explicit module designations. I'll set it to a summary string? The schema: module_designation: \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. It's a single string. Given multiple module designations, I could put a condensed string: \"MS2500 / MV110 HV|LV|LLV and MS2500 / MV210 HV|LV|LLV with SUPERCAP or rechargeable lithium (not replaceable); MS2500 / MV145 HV and MS2500 / MV145 LLV with lithium or alkaline batteries (replaceable)\". Or null since model_family covers it. Hmm — the doc has a \"Designation of the module\" section; I should preserve it. I'll use the condensed string.\n\n**Issuing authority**: name, address_lines: [\"Okružní 31\", \"638 00 Brno\", \"Czech Republic\"], person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\", website: not given. Also signatory RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body — I could add person_title? No, person_title pairs with person_responsible. Jan Kalandra's title isn't given. I'll omit title. Should I include the signer somehow? Not in schema fields; omit.\n\nApplicants address_lines: [\"Via F.lli Gracchi, 27\", \"Cinisello Balsamo (MI)\", \"200 99\", \"Italy\"]. Keep close to doc: \"Via F.lli Gracchi, 27; Cinisello Balsamo - MI; 200 99, Italy\" → lines: [\"Via F.lli Gracchi, 27\", \"Cinisello Balsamo - MI\", \"200 99\", \"Italy\"].\n\nManufacturers: [\"Via Piemonte 2\", \"Montagnana - PD\", \"350 44\", \"Italy\"].\n\nNow for the matrix_tables — the flowrate tables. Structure:\n\n{\n \"name\": \"Basic metrological data (flowrates) — Class 1\",\n \"columns\": [\"dn\", \"r_q3_over_q1\", \"q4\", \"q3\", \"q2\", \"q1\"],\n \"column_units\": [\"mm\", null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\"],\n \"rows\": [...],\n \"description\": \"Flowrates for maximum ratio R, accuracy class 1, types MS2500/MV110, MS2500/MV210, MS2500/MV145\",\n \"footnotes\": []\n}\n\nClass 2 similarly with full DN range.\n\nShould DN unit be \"mm\"? DN 25 means nominal diameter 25 mm. Yes mm.\n\nNow config_level values with structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol, unit_id?}. The schema for config_level: values[]: {condition, value}. I'll include unit_symbol too as rules require structured values. I'll add unit_id: null everywhere per rule 3 (\"Leave unit_id: null unless certain\"). Should I include unit_id in every value object? The rule says every characteristic value MUST be an object with value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers. OK, I'll include unit_id: null and footnote_markers: [] (or omit empty arrays — the shape shows footnote_markers with markers; I'll include empty array or omit. I'll include \"footnote_markers\": [] where empty? To be safe include; or omit when empty. The shape example includes it. I'll include only when non-empty to reduce noise... Hmm \"MUST be an object with this shape\" — I'll include unit_id: null and footnote_markers: [] consistently. That's verbose but compliant.\n\nLet me now also handle q1/q2/q3/q4 relationships: q2 = 1.6 × q1, q4 = 1.25 × q3 — captured as type_level ratios.\n\nLet me also double-check MPE representation. I'll go:\n\ntype_level:\n\"maximum_permitted_error_lower_flowrate_zone\": {\"value\": \"±3 (class 1); ±5 (class 2)\", \"unit_symbol\": \"%\", \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []}\n\nHmm, actually maybe cleaner as config_level with axis accuracy_class:\n{\"attribute\": \"maximum_permitted_error_lower_flowrate_zone\", \"axis\": \"accuracy_class\", \"values\": [{\"condition\": \"class 1\", \"value\": -3...}]}. No — MPE ±3 means magnitude 3. I'll keep type_level strings. Fine.\n\nUpper zone: \"±1 (class 1); ±2 (class 2) / ±2 (class 1); ±3 (class 2)\" — the two rows. Value string: \"±1 (class 1); ±2 (class 2); ±2 (class 1); ±3 (class 2)\". Hmm ambiguous. Keep with newline→\"; \". OK.\n\nNow model_level entries with 8 models each — let me write them.\n\nModel IDs: \"MS2500/MV110 HV\", \"MS2500/MV110 LV\", \"MS2500/MV110 LLV\", \"MS2500/MV210 HV\", \"MS2500/MV210 LV\", \"MS2500/MV210 LLV\", \"MS2500/MV145 HV\", \"MS2500/MV145 LLV\".\n\nmodel_family.models entries with attributes:\n- HV (MV110/MV210): power_supply: \"100–240 V AC mains\", backup: \"SUPERCAP or rechargeable lithium battery 3.7 V DC 5200 mAh (not replaceable)\"\n- MV145: backup: \"lithium 3.6 V or alkaline 9 V batteries (replaceable)\"\n\nI'll put brief attributes and rely on model_level for details.\n\nmodel_level:\n\n1. {\"attribute\": \"power_supply_voltage\", \"unit_symbol\": \"V\", \"values\": [\n {model MV110 HV: {min:100,max:240}}, MV210 HV same, MV110 LV {24,36}, MV210 LV, MV110 LLV {12,48}, MV210 LLV {12,48}, MV145 HV {100,240}, MV145 LLV {12,48}]}\n\n2. {\"attribute\": \"power_supply_type\", \"values\": [HV: \"AC mains\", LV: \"AC/DC mains\", LLV: \"DC mains\", MV145 HV: \"AC mains\", MV145 LLV: \"DC mains\"]}\n\n3. {\"attribute\": \"power_supply_frequency\", \"unit_symbol\": \"Hz\": HV {45,66}; LV \"0 (DC) or 45–66\" string; LLV 0; MV145 HV {45,66}; MV145 LLV \"DC\"}\n\n4. internal_backup_source: MV110/MV210: \"SUPERCAP or rechargeable lithium, 3.7 V DC, 5200 mAh\"; MV145: \"Lithium 3.6 V or alkaline 9 V (replaceable)\"\n\n5. backup_battery_replaceable: MV110/MV210: false; MV145: true. Good structured boolean. Value type: number|string|{min,max} per rules... boolean not listed. Hmm. The schema example earlier had characteristics values. Boolean may be fine but rules say value: number | {min,max} | string. I'll use string \"not replaceable\"/\"replaceable\". Safer.\n\n6. minimum_battery_life_time_internal_backup: strings per model.\n\n7. software_version: strings.\n\n8. crc_checksum_crc32: strings.\n\n9. cut_off is same for all → type_level.\n\nAlso battery life differs only between MV110/MV210 group and MV145 group; still list per model.\n\nNow lengths config — expand per DN:\n\nDN25–50: 200\nDN65–80: 200\nDN100: 250, DN125: 250, DN150: 300\nDN200: 350, DN250: 450, DN300: 500, DN350: 550\nDN400–600: 600 (single value \"600\" spans the group → all 600)\nDN700: 700, DN800: 800, DN900: 900, DN1000: 1000\nDN1200: 1200, DN1400: 1400, DN1500: 1500, DN1600: 1600\nDN1800: 1800, DN2000: 2000\n\nWait, DN400 group cell was \"600\" alone — for DNs 400;450;500;600. So 600 mm each. OK.\n\nIndicating range: DN25–50: 99999; DN65–80: 999999; DN100–150: 999999; DN200–350: 9999999; DN400–2000: 999999999. Units m³.\n\nResolution: DN25–50: 0.0001; DN65–80: 0.001; DN100–150: 0.001; DN200–350: 0.01; DN400–500: 0.01; DN600: 0.1; DN700–1000: 0.1; DN1200–2000: 1.\n\nNote: resolution table header says \"testing mode/user mode\" — single values per group. OK.\n\nIndicating range also \"testing mode/user mode\".\n\nNow let me also decide: put q3_over_q1 as config_level axis \"nominal_diameter and accuracy_class\":\nvalues:\n- {condition: \"class 1, DN 25–600\", value: 250}\n- {condition: \"class 2, DN 25–600\", value: 400}\n- {condition: \"class 2, DN 700\", value: 250}\n- {condition: \"class 2, DN 800\", value: 160}\n- {condition: \"class 2, DN 900\", value: 160}\n- {condition: \"class 2, DN 1000\", value: 100}\n- {condition: \"class 2, DN 1200–1600\", value: 80}\n- {condition: \"class 2, DN 1800–2000\", value: 40}\n\nThat matches both the DN400-2000 table and the flowrate tables. \n\nFootnote: the doc footnote \"1)\" text: \"The ratio Q₃ / Q₁ shall be chosen according to paragraph 4.1.4 of OIML R 49-1:2013\" — clean subscripts: \"The ratio Q3/Q1 shall be chosen according to paragraph 4.1.4 of OIML R 49-1:2013\". Marker: \"1\" or \"1)\"? Footnote markers array earlier used \"1)\" style. The doc uses superscript 1. I'll use marker \"1\" in footnotes[] and footnote_markers [\"1\"].\n\nHmm, wait — should the electromagnetic_environment value carry footnote_markers [\"1\"]? The superscript is on the label \"Electromagnetic environment: 1\". Yes, attach [\"1\"].\n\n**Recommendation object:**\n{\"id\": \"R49\", \"edition\": 2013, \"scheme\": \"A\", \"accuracy_classes\": [\"1\", \"2\"]}\n\nAlso maybe amendment: null → omit.\n\n**Certificate number**: \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-22.02\". Include revision? I'll add certificate.revision? Not in schema — skip. Actually, maybe I should include it as part of number? No, number is as printed; revision captured in revision_history. Fine.\n\nNow assemble JSON. Watch JSON validity: no trailing commas, proper quoting, use ³ character fine.\n\nLet me write out the full JSON.\n\nAlso test report roles: 0511-ER-V097-24 → type_evaluation_report. 6015-PT-P0029-25, 6011-PT-SW012-22, 6011-PT-SW009-22, 6011-PT-SW010-23, 6011-PT-SW017-25, 8551-PT-E0261-25 → test_report. 0511-UL-V097-24 → documentation_file.\n\nPage counts: 268, 159, 6, 9, 6, 6, 44.\n\nDates: only ER has date \"2025-09-30\".\n\ndescription for matrix tables class 2 note: \"DN 25–600 with R=400; from DN 700 ratios reduced (see r column)\".\n\nLet me write the certified_type description: \"Electromagnetic water meters consisting of the MS2500 sensor and MV110, MV210 or MV145 electronic converter, in compact or separate version with connecting cable. They measure, memorise and display the volume of water at metering conditions; LCD display with 0.001 m³ volume resolution; impulse and current outputs; RS485 or USB mini-B for legally non-relevant communication.\"\n\nComponents:\n\n[\n {\"role\": \"Sensor (measurement transducer)\", \"type_designations\": [\"MS2500\"], \"alternatives\": null, \"characteristics\": {\"description\": \"Steel body with connecting flanges, one pair of excitation coils, electrodes and connection to the converter\", \"liners\": {\"PTFE\": {\"dn_range\": \"DN 25–DN 2000\", \"max_water_temperature\": {\"value\": 90, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []}}, ...}}\n\nHmm, characteristics{} is free-form. Let me make liners a simpler array-like object:\n\n\"liners\": [\n {\"material\": \"PTFE\", \"dn_range\": \"25–2000\", \"max_water_temperature\": {\"value\": 90, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []}},\n {\"material\": \"Polypropylene\", \"dn_range\": \"25–150\", \"max_water_temperature\": {\"value\": 50, ...}},\n {\"material\": \"Rilsan\", \"dn_range\": \"50–2000\", \"max_water_temperature\": {\"value\": 50, ...}},\n {\"material\": \"Ebonite\", \"dn_range\": \"200–2000\", \"max_water_temperature\": {\"value\": 50, ...}}\n]\n\nThat's nested but acceptable in characteristics{} free-form.\n\nConverter component:\n{\"role\": \"Converter (electronic indicating device)\", \"type_designations\": [\"MV110\", \"MV210\", \"MV145\"], \"alternatives\": \"OR\", \"characteristics\": {\"body\": \"plastic or aluminium\", \"display\": \"LCD showing volume and flow\", \"outputs\": \"impulse output and current output\", \"legally_non_relevant_communication\": \"RS485 or USB mini-B\"}}\n\nPower supply module? The module designations are combos — covered by model_family. OK.\n\nAlso model_family.family_name: null? Or \"MS2500 / MV110; MS2500 / MV210; MS2500 / MV145\"? The family is the water meter type family. I'll set family_name: null (no explicit family name) — models enumerated. Actually maybe \"MS2500\" as family name — the sensor family. The doc doesn't say \"family\". I'll use null per \"explicit family name if any, else null\".\n\nNow write out everything carefully.\n\nFor type_level values, include unit_id: null and footnote_markers: [] consistently? That bloats. The instruction: \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object with this shape\" — includes unit_id and footnote_markers keys. I'll include them everywhere (empty array / null). OK.\n\nLet me also reconsider: values that are strings like \"MAP40\" — unit_symbol null.\n\ntemperature_class config values: strings \"T90; T50; T30\" etc. with note \"from DN50\" — the Rilsan entry: \"T50; T30; from DN50\" means liner available from DN50. I'll encode condition: \"Rilsan liner (from DN 50)\", value: \"T50; T30\". Similarly Ebonite: condition \"Ebonite liner (from DN 200)\", value \"T50; T30\".\n\nConfig-level value shape: {condition, value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. I'll include those keys.\n\nmax_admissible_temperature config:\n- {condition: \"PTFE liner\", value: 90, unit °C}\n- {condition: \"Polypropylene, Rilsan and Ebonite liners\", value: 50, unit °C}\n\nambient_temperature_range: {min: -25, max: 55} °C.\n\nMPE strings: lower: \"±3 (class 1); ±5 (class 2)\", unit \"%\" — but sign: it's ±. Keep \"±3 (class 1); ±5 (class 2)\". Upper: \"±1 (class 1); ±2 (class 2); ±2 (class 1); ±3 (class 2)\" — hmm, better to preserve the two-row distinction: \"row 1: ±1 (class 1); ±2 (class 2) / row 2: ±2 (class 1); ±3 (class 2)\". I'll write: \"±1 (class 1), ±2 (class 2) / ±2 (class 1), ±3 (class 2)\". OK.\n\nAccuracy class type_level: value \"1; 2\" string? Or [1,2]? I'll use \"1 and 2\"? The doc: \"1; 2\". I'll use value: \"1; 2\". Hmm, strings allowed. Fine. Actually recommendation.accuracy_classes covers this too.\n\nReverse flow: \"designed to measure\" (string).\n\nEnvironmental class: \"B, O\" string.\n\nElectromagnetic environment: \"E1; E2\" with footnote [\"1\"].\n\nConnection type: \"Flanged\".\n\nFlow conditioner: \"No\" → flow_conditioner_required: false? Keep string \"No\" as value: \"No\". Or attribute \"flow_conditioner\": \"No\". Fine.\n\nEUT category: \"Electromagnetic water meter\"; EUT case: \"D\".\n\nDisplay volume resolution: 0.001 m³ — from description