rge but OK.\n\nOne more thought on model_level \"power_supply_voltage\" for MV110/MV210 LLV: I decided 12–48. Let me finalize: use {min:12, max:48} and unit V. Hmm... The spec table says 10–48. Two conflicting sources. I'll go with the spec table? Argh. Let me weigh once more: The page 7 table is titled \"Power supply and electrical specification for types MS2500 / MV110; MS2500 / MV210\" — authoritative for these models. It says \"10 – 48 V DC\". Page 3 narrative says 12–48V DC. The MV145 spec table says 12–48 V DC. If the true value were 12–48 for MV110/MV210 too, the table's \"10\" would be a typo/OCR misread of \"12\" (1 vs 2 unlikely in OCR... \"10\" vs \"12\" — GLM OCR could misread). If true value were 10–48, page 3's \"12\" would be a typo. Can't resolve; I'll use the dedicated spec table value 10–48 for MV110/MV210 LLV since that's the model-specific specification table, and note nothing. Hmm, but earlier I leaned the other way. \n\nFinal decision: Use the specification table (10–48 V DC) for MV110/MV210 LLV because it's the dedicated electrical specification for exactly those models. The narrative on page 3 is generic. Hmm, but narrative is also model-specific (\"The water meter MS2500/MV110 LLV and MS2500/MV210 LLV has power supply by main 12 – 48V DC\").\n\nOK coin flip resolved by convention: detailed spec tables usually supersede summary text. Going with 10–48 for MV110/MV210 LLV.\n\nWait, actually — also MV110/MV210 LLV frequency \"0\" — DC. Fine.\n\nMV145 LLV frequency: \"DC\" string.\n\nBackup for MV145: \"3.6 V / Lithium/ 9V Alkaline\".\n\nBattery life MV145: \"Lithium 3 years (1+1) / Alkaline 1 year (nine) both in SMART mode and under proper environmental and working conditions\".\n\nMV110/MV210 battery life: \"SUPER CAP: 1 minutes / Rechargeable Lithium 15 days at reference conditions\" → clean: \"SUPERCAP: 1 minute; rechargeable lithium: 15 days at reference conditions\".\n\nSoftware versions MV110/MV210: \"V.1.05.0000.0004; V.1.06.0000.0004\". MV145: \"V.1.06.0000.0005; V.1.07.0000.0005\" (doc shows \"1.07.0000.0005\" without V — I'll normalize to \"V.1.07.0000.0005\"? Better keep as printed: \"V.1.06.0000.0005; 1.07.0000.0005\". I'll keep as printed.)\n\nCRC MV110/MV210: \"F2476CEF; 40FF6355\". MV145: \"EF726D9C; A1D8736F\".\n\nNow internal_backup_source voltage: 3.7 V DC 5200 mAh (MV110/MV210); MV145: 3.6 V lithium / 9 V alkaline.\n\nNow build the models array with attributes (short descriptors) — 8 entries.\n\nLet me draft the final JSON mentally and then write it.\n\nTop-level:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-22.02\",\n    \"revision\": \"5\",  // not in schema — omit? I'll omit to follow schema strictly. Hmm... Actually including revision seems useful; schema says don't fabricate fields not present. The schema listing doesn't forbid extra fields, but to be safe I'll omit.\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"page_total\": 12,\n    \"member_state\": \"Czech Republic\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2025-10-03\"\n  },\n  ...\n}\n\nActually, let me add \"revision\": \"5\"? The rules say schema fields; extra fields risk validation failure. Omit.\n\nissuing_authority: {\n \"name\": \"Czech Metrology Institute\",\n \"address_lines\": [\"Okružní 31\", \"638 00 Brno\", \"Czech Republic\"],\n \"person_responsible\": \"Jan Kalandra\"\n}\n\nHmm — should I include \"Head of Certification Body: RNDr. Pavel Klenovský\"? person_title would then not match Jan Kalandra. I'll add nothing else. Actually maybe person_responsible should be the signer? Page 1 explicitly labels \"Person responsible: Jan Kalandra\". Keep Jan Kalandra.\n\napplicants: [{\n \"name\": \"ISOIL INDUSTRIA SPA\",\n \"address_lines\": [\"Via F.lli Gracchi, 27\", \"Cinisello Balsamo - MI\", \"200 99\", \"Italy\"]\n}]\n\nmanufacturers: [{\n \"name\": \"Hemina SPA\",\n \"address_lines\": [\"Via Piemonte 2\", \"Montagnana - PD\", \"350 44\", \"Italy\"]\n}]\n\ncertified_type: {\n \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n \"type_designations\": [\"MS2500 / MV110\", \"MS2500 / MV210\", \"MS2500 / MV145\"],\n \"module_designation\": \"MS2500 / MV110 HV, LV, LLV and MS2500 / MV210 HV, LV, LLV with SUPERCAP or rechargeable lithium battery (not replaceable); MS2500 / MV145 HV and MS2500 / MV145 LLV with lithium or alkaline batteries (replaceable)\",\n \"description\": \"Electromagnetic water meters designed to measure, memorise and display the volume of water at metering conditions, consisting of the MS2500 sensor and the MV110, MV210 or MV145 electronic converter in compact or separate version with connecting cable. Sensor: steel body with flanges, excitation coils, electrodes, with PTFE, polypropylene, Rilsan or ebonite liner. Converter: plastic or aluminium housing with LCD display (volume resolution 0.001 m³), impulse and current outputs, and RS485 or USB mini-B for legally non-relevant communication.\"\n}\n\nmodel_family: {\n \"family_name\": null,\n \"models\": [\n   {\"model_id\": \"MS2500/MV110 HV\", \"label\": \"HV\", \"attributes\": {\"power_supply\": \"100–240 V AC mains\", \"backup_source\": \"SUPERCAP or rechargeable lithium (not replaceable)\"}},\n   ...\n ]\n}\n\nHmm label — schema: model_id, label, attributes{}. label could be the module designation text. Let me set label to full module designation string, e.g. \"MS2500 / MV110 HV + SUPERCAP or Rechargeable Lithium (not replaceable)\". For MV145: \"MS2500 / MV145 HV and/or Lithium or Alkaline Batteries (replaceable)\".\n\n8 models:\n1. MS2500/MV110 HV — label \"MS2500 / MV110 HV + SUPERCAP or Rechargeable Lithium (not replaceable)\"\n2. MS2500/MV110 LV — \"MS2500 / MV110 LV + SUPERCAP or Rechargeable Lithium (not replaceable)\"\n3. MS2500/MV110 LLV — \"MS2500 / MV110 LLV + SUPERCAP or Rechargeable Lithium (not replaceable)\"\n4. MS2500/MV210 HV — \"MS2500 / MV210 HV + SUPERCAP or Rechargeable Lithium (not replaceable)\"\n5. MS2500/MV210 LV — ...\n6. MS2500/MV210 LLV — ...\n7. MS2500/MV145 HV — \"MS2500 / MV145 HV and/or Lithium or Alkaline Batteries (replaceable)\"\n8. MS2500/MV145 LLV — \"MS2500 / MV145 LLV and/or Lithium or Alkaline Batteries (replaceable)\"\n\nattributes minimal: maybe omit attributes{} or give {\"converter\": \"MV110\", \"power_supply_variant\": \"HV\"}. I'll include small attributes.\n\ncomponents: [\n sensor, converter\n]\n\ncharacteristics: {\n \"type_level\": {...},\n \"model_level\": [...],\n \"config_level\": [...]\n}\n\nmatrix_tables: [class1, class2]\n\nrecommendation: {\"id\": \"R49\", \"edition\": 2013, \"scheme\": \"A\", \"accuracy_classes\": [\"1\", \"2\"]}\n\ntest_reports: 8 entries.\n\nrevision_history: 6 entries.\n\nfootnotes: [{marker: \"1\", text: \"The ratio Q3/Q1 shall be chosen according to paragraph 4.1.4 of OIML R 49-1:2013\"}]\n\nNow, type_level full list with structured values:\n\n1. \"measuring_principle\": {\"value\": \"Electromagnetic water meter\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []}\n2. \"accuracy_class\": {\"value\": \"1; 2\", ...}\n3. \"q2_over_q1\": {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": null, ...}\n4. \"q4_over_q3\": {\"value\": 1.25, ...}\n5. \"maximum_permitted_error_lower_flowrate_zone\": {\"value\": \"±3 (class 1); ±5 (class 2)\", \"unit_symbol\": \"%\", ...}\n6. \"maximum_permitted_error_upper_flowrate_zone\": {\"value\": \"±1 (class 1); ±2 (class 2) / ±2 (class 1); ±3 (class 2)\", \"unit_symbol\": \"%\", ...}\n7. \"water_pressure_class\": {\"value\": \"MAP40\", ...}\n8. \"pressure_loss_class\": {\"value\": \"Δp40\", ...}\n9. \"reverse_flow\": {\"value\": \"designed to measure\", ...}\n10. \"environmental_class\": {\"value\": \"B, O\", ...}\n11. \"electromagnetic_environment\": {\"value\": \"E1; E2\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"1\"], ...}\n12. \"ambient_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -25, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", ...}\n13. \"maximum_admissible_pressure\": {\"value\": 4, \"unit_symbol\": \"MPa\", ...}\n14. \"orientation_limitation\": {\"value\": \"any\", ...}\n15. \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\": {\"value\": \"U0D0\", ...}\n16. \"installation_sensitivity_class\": {\"value\": \"U0 D0\", ...}\n17. \"connection_type\": {\"value\": \"Flanged\", ...}\n18. \"minimum_straight_length_inlet_pipe\": {\"value\": 0, \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\", ...}\n19. \"minimum_straight_length_outlet_pipe\": {\"value\": 0, \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\", ...}\n20. \"flow_conditioner\": {\"value\": \"No\", ...} — maybe attribute \"flow_conditioner_required\". Keep \"flow_conditioner\": \"No\".\n21. \"eut_test_category\": {\"value\": \"Electromagnetic water meter\", ...}\n22. \"eut_case\": {\"value\": \"D\", ...}\n23. \"cut_off\": {\"value\": 0.2, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\", ...}\n24. \"cut_off_reference\": {\"value\": \"Q3\", ...}\n25. \"empty_pipe_detection\": {\"value\": \"on\", ...}\n26. \"display_volume_resolution\": {\"value\": 0.001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\", ...}\n\nconfig_level:\n1. temperature_class (axis: liner): conditions as above, values strings.\n2. maximum_admissible_temperature (axis: liner): PTFE 90 °C; \"polypropylene, Rilsan, ebonite\" 50 °C.\n3. q3_over_q1_ratio (axis: nominal_diameter and accuracy_class): 8 entries.\n4. indicating_range (axis: nominal_diameter): 5 entries.\n5. indicating_device_resolution (axis: nominal_diameter): 8 entries.\n6. length (axis: nominal_diameter): per DN entries — 27 entries? That's a lot. Could group: DN 25–50: 200; DN 65–80: 200; DN 100: 250; DN 125: 250; DN 150: 300; DN 200: 350; DN 250: 450; DN 300: 500; DN 350: 550; DN 400–600: 600; DN 700–1000: 700–1000 respectively... For DN700–1000 the length equals DN numerically: 700,800,900,1000. And 1200–1600: equal to DN. 1800,2000: equal. So entries: DN700:700, DN800:800, DN900:900, DN1000:1000, DN1200:1200, DN1400:1400, DN1500:1500, DN1600:1600, DN1800:1800, DN2000:2000. I'll expand all individually — total entries: 4 (25–50 group → expand: 25:200,32:200,40:200,50:200) hmm expansion to individual DNs = 27 entries. It's verbose but precise. Alternatively group where uniform. I'll do grouped conditions:\n\n- {\"condition\": \"DN 25–50\", \"value\": 200}\n- {\"condition\": \"DN 65–80\", \"value\": 200}\n- {\"condition\": \"DN 100\", \"value\": 250}\n- {\"condition\": \"DN 125\", \"value\": 250}\n- {\"condition\": \"DN 150\", \"value\": 300}\n- {\"condition\": \"DN 200\", \"value\": 350}\n- {\"condition\": \"DN 250\", \"value\": 450}\n- {\"condition\": \"DN 300\", \"value\": 500}\n- {\"condition\": \"DN 350\", \"value\": 550}\n- {\"condition\": \"DN 400–600\", \"value\": 600}\n- {\"condition\": \"DN 700\", \"value\": 700}\n- {\"condition\": \"DN 800\", \"value\": 800}\n- {\"condition\": \"DN 900\", \"value\": 900}\n- {\"condition\": \"DN 1000\", \"value\": 1000}\n- {\"condition\": \"DN 1200\", \"value\": 1200}\n- {\"condition\": \"DN 1400\", \"value\": 1400}\n- {\"condition\": \"DN 1500\", \"value\": 1500}\n- {\"condition\": \"DN 1600\", \"value\": 1600}\n- {\"condition\": \"DN 1800\", \"value\": 1800}\n- {\"condition\": \"DN 2000\", \"value\": 2000}\n\nunit mm.\n\nindicating_range entries:\n- DN 25–50: 99999 m³\n- DN 65–80: 999999\n- DN 100–150: 999999\n- DN 200–350: 9999999\n- DN 400–2000: 999999999\n\nresolution entries:\n- DN 25–50: 0.0001\n- DN 65–80: 0.001\n- DN 100–150: 0.001\n- DN 200–350: 0.01\n- DN 400–500: 0.01\n- DN 600: 0.1\n- DN 700–1000: 0.1\n- DN 1200–2000: 1\nunit m³.\n\nmodel_level: 8 attributes × 8 models — sizeable. Let me write with all 8 models per attribute.\n\npower_supply_voltage values:\n- {model: \"MS2500/MV110 HV\", value: {min:100,max:240}, unit_symbol:\"V\"}\n- MV110 LV: {24,36} V\n- MV110 LLV: {min:10,max:48} V\n- MV210 HV: {100,240}\n- MV210 LV: {24,36}\n- MV210 LLV: {10,48}\n- MV145 HV: {100,240}\n- MV145 LLV: {12,48}\n\nEach value object also needs unit_id & footnote_markers per strict shape? The model_level example in rules shows {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} — minimal shape without unit at value level (unit at attribute level). The rule 2 strict shape applies to characteristic values; the model_level example shows simpler. I'll follow the model_level example: values entries with model + value (+ footnote_markers when needed). Units at the attribute entry level via \"unit_symbol\". For power_supply_type (no unit), fine.\n\npower_supply_frequency:\nunit Hz:\n- HV (MV110): {min:45,max:66}\n- LV: \"0 (DC) or 45–66\" — string. Hmm \"0 (DC) …45 – 66 Hz\". I'll write value: \"0 (DC) or 45–66\".\n- LLV: 0\n- MV210 same as MV110 entries.\n- MV145 HV: {45,66}\n- MV145 LLV: \"DC\" string, unit null? attribute unit Hz; value \"DC\". OK.\n\npower_supply_type (no unit):\n- HV: \"AC mains\", LV: \"AC/DC mains\", LLV: \"DC mains\", MV145 HV \"AC mains\", MV145 LLV \"DC mains\".\n\ninternal_backup_source (string):\n- MV110/MV210 (all 6): \"SUPERCAP or rechargeable lithium battery, 3.7 V DC, 5200 mAh\"\n- MV145 (2): \"Lithium 3.6 V or alkaline 9 V battery\"\n\nbackup_battery_replaceable? Skip (encoded in source string).\n\nminimum_battery_life_time_internal_backup:\n- MV110/MV210: \"SUPERCAP: 1 minute; rechargeable lithium: 15 days at reference conditions\"\n- MV145: \"Lithium: 3 years (1+1); alkaline: 1 year (nine), in SMART mode under proper environmental and working conditions\"\n\nsoftware_version:\n- MV110/MV210: \"V.1.05.0000.0004; V.1.06.0000.0004\"\n- MV145: \"V.1.06.0000.0005; 1.07.0000.0005\"\n\ncrc_checksum_crc32:\n- MV110/MV210: \"F2476CEF; 40FF6355\"\n- MV145: \"EF726D9C; A1D8736F\"\n\nThat's 6 model_level attributes. Each with 8 values (voltage/freq/type) or 8 (backup/life/sw/crc). Good.\n\nAlso frequency attribute: include unit_symbol \"Hz\" at attribute level; for LLV value 0 and MV145 LLV \"DC\".\n\nNow matrix tables with all rows. Include \"footnotes\": [].\n\nLet me also decide whether to include \"description\" for tables: yes short.\n\nCheck: should I include the DN-group \"Q3/Q1\" page-6 table as matrix table too? Already encoded in config_level q3_over_q1_ratio. Fine.\n\nLabel description stuff (page 12): example label — skip, or mention IP68/IP67 in description? The label shows IP 68 (sensor) and IP 67 (converter) as example content — these are label examples, not necessarily certified characteristics. Skip to avoid fabrication. Hmm — could include in components characteristics \"ingress_protection (label example): sensor IP68, converter IP67\". It's \"label content\" example. I'll skip.\n\nEUT marking \"Environmental classification\" etc. — skip.\n\nNow finalize JSON. Be careful about \"m³/h\" strings and °C. Also \"Δp40\" — fine.\n\nOne more check — scheme field for recommendation: \"A\". And certificate.scheme \"A\".\n\nproject_number: not present — omit.\n\noiml_issuer_id: omit both places.\n\nLet me write the final JSON now, ensuring valid syntax. I'll write compactly but readably.\n\nAlso note: for model_level attribute entries, structure: {\"attribute\": \"...\", \"unit_symbol\": \"V\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"...\", \"value\": {...}}]}. Add \"unit_id\": null at attribute level? The example didn't include it. I'll include unit_symbol only, matching example.\n\nFor config_level: {\"attribute\": \"...\", \"axis\": \"...\", \"values\": [{\"condition\": \"...\", \"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": \"...\"}]}.\n\nWrite it all out.\n\nDouble-check revision dates: Rev 0: 26 May 2022 → 2022-05-26. Rev 1: 13 September 2022 → 2022-09-13. Rev 2: 2 February 2023 → 2023-02-02. Rev 3: 20 October 2023 → 2023-10-20. Rev 4: 28 May 2024 → 2024-05-28. Rev 5: 3 October 2025 → 2025-10-03.\n\nType evaluation report date: 30th September 2025 → 2025-09-30.\n\nOK, compose.",
